From 44976cbc215fd9067c0cc5b86b636249f1fd1f44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:40:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Look up definitions without rescanning the module per
 query

macOS 27.2 draws its first frame with CoreAnimation's uber fragment
shader: 94 KB of AIR that inlines to one function of 5,200 blocks and
62,000 instructions. Translating it took 17 s on the host's little cores
(8.3 s in a guest on the big ones), and most of that was linear searches
repeated per instruction or per loop:

- the continue-merge repair walked every block for every loop header,
  and rebuilt the dominator tree after each one-operand retarget;
- the wrapper collapse, the private-placeholder neutralizer and three
  load planners called type_def_of or value_result_type, each a scan of
  the globals or of the whole module, once per instruction in loops that
  change nothing those scans read;
- the helper inliner cloned every global into a fresh map for each
  inlined pointer instruction, and regrew its result-type map per call;
- exact_raw_word_fact searched every function for a pointer's
  definition on each load the raw-word rewrite considered.

Each now reads a map built for that loop. A map answers what the scan
it replaces answered: the first definition in module order, and for the
inliner the last one, synthesized globals first. The raw-word rewrite
keeps its map in step with every instruction it inserts or changes,
stops using it at the first id defined twice, and debug builds compare
each answer with the scan. A merge retarget changes no branch, so it
keeps the dominator tree; a repair that adds a block rebuilds it.

The SPIR-V and reflection are byte-identical for that shader and for
144 other AIR files from macOS 13.6 to 27.2 guests. The shader now
translates in 6.1 s instead of 17.0 s on the little cores, and in 2.9 s
instead of 7.9 s on a big one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
